    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111739": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Assistant Professor - Applied Insect Chemist Assistant Professor - Applied Insect Chemist Faculty Position Available: Applied Insect Chemist Department of Entomology, Cornell University Position: Assistant Professor, Tenure-track. Location: Geneva, NY, USA. The academic home for this position is the Department of Entomology in the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences, housed on the Geneva campus of Cornell University. The Department of Entomology at Cornell University is seeking applications for a 9-month, full-time, tenure-track position in the area of Applied Insect Chemistry. The successful candidate is expected to establish an externally funded and internationally recognized research program in insect, plant, and agricultural chemistry that directly contributes to specialty crop protection. Insect chemical ecology plays a pivotal role in multiple disciplines, including biochemical synthesis, toxicology, behavior and neurobiology, that contribute towards successful management of agricultural, invasive, and animal pests. Advances in chemical analysis support breakthroughs in specialty crop pest management. We therefore seek candidates with strong expertise in chemistry coupled with deep motivation to develop implementable insect pest management tools for New York agriculture. Engagement with other disciplines is essential to achieve the mission of Cornell AgriTech to improve the health of people, the environment and the economy of New York. The successful candidate is therefore expected to establish collaborations with scientists across Cornell’s campuses as well as those outside of Cornell. Outstanding research scholarship is expected, as is excellence in extension, translation of knowledge, and inclusive mentorship of students. We seek colleagues with a record demonstrating success and promise across these areas, and who will be supported by and contribute to a vibrant culture of inclusive excellence at Cornell University. As such, candidates are expected to engage in service and leadership activities within the department, college, university, and relevant professional societies. The candidate will become a member of the Cornell AgriTech community which maintains close connections with New York’s specialty crop industries. Responsibilities : This position has an effort split of 70% research and 30% extension. RESEARCH (70%): The successful candidate is expected to establish an internationally recognized research program in insect chemical biology and pest management, using a combination of chemical, behavioral or toxicological approaches to investigate the interactions between agriculturally relevant insects and plants.  These approaches should be supported with field evaluations and developed into applied control strategies relevant to specialty crops in New York. Collaborative projects with industry should evaluate technologies for use by New York specialty crop growers and food producers while contributing to agricultural innovation and economic development. EXTENSION (30%): The successful candidate is expected to build an integrated extension program that translates research innovations into improved pest monitoring and pest management tools for New York’s specialty crop industry. The successful candidate will utilize multiple modes of communication in their extension program including extension presentations, newsletter and trade-journal articles, social media, fact sheets, webinars and field demonstrations. This may include but is not limited to research-based information about pesticides and other agricultural chemicals, insect behavior-modifying compounds, and plant chemical signaling. We anticipate that the candidate will work closely with faculty, Cornell Cooperative Extension educators, the Cornell Integrated Pest Management Program, commodity organizations, government agencies, and technology developers to address insect-related problems within the specialty crop industry and advance the responsible development and implementation of effective pest-management solutions. TEACHING: There is no formal teaching assignment with this position. However, there is an expectation that the successful candidate will contribute occasional guest lectures in courses like chemical ecology and those pertaining to pest management. There is also an opportunity to occasionally offer graduate seminars in your area of expertise. MENTORING: There is an expectation that the successful candidate will mentor graduate students, postdoctoral associates, and staff in their lab. There will also be an opportunity to mentor undergraduate students as part of Cornell AgriTech’s Summer Research Scholars Program , a highly successful and unique internship program that provides hands-on research experience for undergraduate students from diverse backgrounds. Additionally, the candidate will be encouraged to provide training and mentorship for graduate students and postdocs on the use of analytical chemistry equipment housed within the lab. The candidate will be responsible for establishing an inclusive, equitable, and productive working environment, and respectful mentor-mentee relationships that support the needs and goals of mentees. Department Affiliation : The successful candidate will be a tenure-line faculty member of the Cornell University College of Agriculture and Life Sciences and will be based in the Department of Entomology in Geneva, NY. QUALIFICATIONS : Ph.D. degree in chemistry, biology or an agricultural discipline that has a strong biological chemistry and entomology background. Working knowledge of the methods and instrumentation used for collection and chemical analysis of entomological, botanical, and other agriculturally relevant compounds.  A commitment to advancing agriculture by participating in multidisciplinary team-based research and extension programs is essential. Preferred qualifications include postdoctoral experience, successful collaborative experience, and ability to communicate effectively with diverse groups, including students, colleagues, and industry stakeholders. Application and Starting Date : The anticipated starting date is August 1, 2027, or as negotiated. Qualified applicants should submit 1) a cover letter briefly summarizing background, qualifications, and interest in the position, 2) a Curriculum Vitae, 3) a research statement outlining experience, interests, and goals, 4) an extension statement outlining experience, interests, and goals, 5) copies of three relevant publications, and 6) names and contact information for three references. Materials should be submitted online to: https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32931 Applications received by December 8, 2026, by . 11:59pm EST will be given full consideration. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. This is an in-person position. The successful candidate will be expected to live within commuting distance of Geneva, NY. Relocation assistance may be provided. Finalists for this position will be asked to sign an affidavit that they have not been found to have violated employer policies at their previous institutions. Inquiries may be directed to: Dr. Kyle Wickings Department of Entomology Cornell University Geneva, NY 14456 Email: kgw37@cornell.edu Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointment to academic positions requires candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. We seek a colleague with an outstanding record demonstrating success and promise across all these areas, and who will be supported by and contribute to a vibrant culture of inclusive excellence at Cornell. Learn how CALS fosters an environment where faculty are supported, valued, and empowered to make meaningful contributions throughout their careers. In compliance with New York's Pay Transparency Law, the annual base salary range for this assistant professor position is $90,000 - $135,000. This pay range represents base pay (for 9 months). Cornell University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Cornell University seeks to meet the needs of dual career couples, has a Dual Career program, and is a member of the Upstate New York Higher Education Recruitment Consortium to assist with dual career searches including positions available in higher education in the upstate New York area. The new faculty member will join a collaborative, interdisciplinary community at Cornell University.  Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League and Land-grant university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students, and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery, and engagement. Cornell’s regional and global presence includes state-wide Cornell Cooperative Extension programs and offices in all counties and boroughs, global partnerships with institutions and communities engaged in life-changing research and education, the medical college’s campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, and the Cornell Tech campus on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. With a founding principle of “…any person…any study,” Cornell is an equal opportunity employer . Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-15 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111696": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Teaching Professor",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Assistant/Associate Teaching Professor Assistant/Associate Teaching Professor The Department of German Studies at Cornell University invites applications for a full-time Assistant/Associate Teaching Professor Position beginning July 1, 2027. This non-tenure-track position is for an initial three- or five-year contract with renewal at three- or five-year intervals contingent upon successful performance reviews. The usual teaching load consists of 3 courses per semester. Our new colleague will work in a small team with the perspective of taking on the role of language program director (LPD) on a rotating basis. The language program is based on the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages. Responsibilities include: 1) contributing to the language teaching needs of the department; 2) educating and supervising graduate student instructors; 3) participating in curricular development; and 4) promoting and supporting the German undergraduate program. Required qualifications: o    Advanced degree in Applied Linguistics, Applied Language Pedagogy, German as a Foreign Language (DaF), German Studies, or equivalent (Ph.D. or M.A. with additional language teaching experience); o    superior proficiency in German (C1) and English; o    commitment to continued professional development; o    at least two years of prior college-level language teaching post degree, and supervising experience o    ability to develop and teach undergraduate language courses in areas of departmental interests and curricular outreach, including “German Business Culture\" o    demonstrated initiative in creating a thriving language community inside and outside the classroom; o    commitment to innovation in teaching and state-of-the-art learning and language acquisition theories; o    excellent organizational, communicative and collaborative skills. Pay range: $70,000 - $85,000 Actual salary offers in the College of Arts & Sciences will be based on education, experience, discipline, and relevant skills. Deadline for applications is November 10, 2026. Electronic submission of applications is required. Submit a letter of application, a statement of teaching philosophy, CV, at least three sample course syllabi, and three letters of recommendation. Disclosure: Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-14 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
