    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111490": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Teaching Professor in Operations, Technology, and Information Management, SC Johnson College of Business",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Assistant or Associate Teaching Professor in Operations, Technology, and Information Management, SC Johnson College of Business Assistant or Associate Teaching Professor in Operations, Technology, and Information Management, SC Johnson College of Business The Cornell SC Johnson College of Business invites applicants for a non-tenure Assistant Teaching Professor (3-year renewable term) or Associate Teaching Professor (5-year renewable term) position in the Operations, Technology, and Information Management area, with a school affiliation in the Cornell Peter and Stephanie Nolan School of Hotel Administration. Candidates would be appointed at the rank that is appropriate given their experience and performance. The intended start date is July 1, 2027. The position will be in-person teaching based in Ithaca, NY. The candidate should have extensive knowledge of the hospitality industry and at least four years of relevant industry experience in food and beverage management.  This can include restaurant management, hotel food and beverage operations, catering, event management, and/or beverage management.  Experience in catering and event management is preferred. The ideal candidate will possess a PhD in Hospitality or a closely related field and relevant teaching experience. Candidates holding a Masters’ degree in Hospitality or a closely related field and relevant teaching experience will also be considered. The Cornell SC Johnson College of Business (established on July 1, 2016) combines three Schools:  the Samuel Curtis Johnson Graduate School of Management (Johnson), the Charles H. Dyson School of Applied Economics and Management (Dyson), and the Nolan School of Hotel Administration (Nolan). It is expected that our faculty teach across multiple schools within the College of Business. To be considered for this position, apply at [https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32863] . For full consideration, please ensure that your application is complete by December 1, 2026. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position has been filled. Applications received after December 2, 2026 will be reviewed if the position has not been filled. The following materials are required: 1.  Letter of interest, with clear indication of position being applied for; 2.  Curriculum vitae; 3.  Up to three research papers or other representative samples of scholarship; 4.  Three recommendation letters; 5.  Report of courses taught and evidence of teaching effectiveness; and 6.  Any other supporting documents, such as hospitality industry certifications, curriculum development materials Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointment to academic positions requires candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. In compliance with New York’s Pay Transparency Act, the minimum annual base salary range for this position is $105,000 - $280,000.  This pay range represents base pay only (for 9 months, paid over 12 months) and excludes any additional items such as incentives, bonuses, or other items. Cornell University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-07 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111470": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Postdoctoral Associate Postdoctoral Associate Academic Job Description Department of Animal Science Postdoctoral Associate The College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS) is a pioneer of purpose-driven science and Cornell University’s second largest college. We work across disciplines to tackle the challenges of our time through world-renowned research, education, and outreach. The questions we probe and the answers we seek focus on three overlapping concerns: We believe that achieving next-generation scientific breakthroughs requires an understanding of the world’s complex, interlocking systems. We believe that access to nutritious food and a healthy environment is a fundamental human right. We believe that ensuring a prosperous global future depends on the ability to support local people and communities everywhere. By working in and across multiple scientific areas, CALS can address challenges and opportunities of the greatest relevance, here in New York, across the nation, and around the world. Position Function The Postdoctoral Associate will conduct independent research in the Laboratory of Integrative Physiology (Selvaraj Lab) addressing fundamental questions at the intersection of cell biology and physiology. Research will focus on mechanisms underlying cellular reprogramming, pluripotency, gamete maturation, fertilization, and early embryo development using cellular and large-animal experimental systems. Related research may include investigation of cellular signaling, gene and protein function, and physiological approaches to improving in vitro fertilization and pregnancy success. The Postdoctoral Associate will design and conduct experiments, critically analyze and interpret data, and develop or adapt experimental approaches to address emerging scientific questions. The individual will be expected to independently advance research projects, contribute intellectually and experimentally to collaborative studies, and communicate research findings through presentations and peer-reviewed publications. Projects may evolve based on experimental findings, emerging research questions, and the scientific interests and expertise of the individual. The Postdoctoral Associate will also participate in the broader intellectual activities of the laboratory and will have opportunities to mentor and train undergraduate researchers. Anticipated Division of Time 80% - Research Independently conduct research addressing fundamental questions in cell biology and physiology. Design, perform, analyze, and adapt experiments related to the laboratory’s research interests, including cellular reprogramming, pluripotency, gamete maturation, fertilization, early embryo development, signaling, gene discovery, and protein function. Develop and optimize experimental approaches as necessary to address emerging research questions. 15% – Data Analysis and Scientific Communication Critically analyze and interpret experimental data, maintain rigorous documentation of experimental results, integrate findings with relevant scientific literature, and communicate research findings through scientific presentations, manuscripts, and publications. 5% – Mentoring and Collaborative Activities Contribute to collaborative research within the laboratory and provide research training and mentorship to undergraduate students as appropriate. Requirements • Ph.D. in the life sciences with a background in cell and molecular biology. • Experience or understanding in reproductive biology, cell fate reprogramming and systems physiology is preferred but not required. • Ability to rapidly learn, critically evaluate, and integrate information across disciplines, while maintaining awareness of both foundational and emerging knowledge. • Ability to independently design, conduct, troubleshoot, and adapt experiments to address scientific questions. • Strong analytical and communication skills, including rigorous documentation, scientific writing and publication, and the ability to lead projects independently while working collaboratively. Supervision Exercised There are no supervisory responsibilities for this position. The Postdoctoral Associate will have opportunities to mentor and train undergraduate students but will not have formal supervisory responsibilities. Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointments to academic positions require candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. CALS hiring range for this title is University minimum to $63,480.00. College of Agriculture and Life Sciences Life. Changing. Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League and Land-grant university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students, and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery, and engagement. Cornell’s regional and global presence includes state-wide Cornell Cooperative Extension programs and offices in all counties and boroughs, global partnerships with institutions and communities engaged in life-changing research and education, the medical college’s campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, and the Cornell Tech campus on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. With a founding principle of “any person, any study,” Cornell is an equal opportunity employer . Pay Range: $62,232.00 - $88,745.00 Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-07 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111375": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track or Tenured Faculty Position in Applied and Engineering Physics Cornell University",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Tenure-Track or Tenured Faculty Position in Applied and Engineering Physics Cornell University Tenure-Track or Tenured Faculty Position in Applied and Engineering Physics Cornell University The School of Applied and Engineering Physics (AEP) at Cornell University seeks applications for a tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professors.  Candidates must be able to demonstrate the ability to develop a highly successful and internationally recognized independent research program and to participate effectively in the teaching of our core physics curriculum at the advanced undergraduate level. Research areas of interest include biophysics and biotechnology, optics and photonics, nanoscience and nanotechnology, novel instrumentation methods, quantum information science and technology, new energy technologies, and materials physics.  Applicants could approach these areas from experimental or theoretical perspectives including computational or machine learning.  The successful applicant can expect a highly competitive level of support for the start-up of their research program. Considerable institutional resources at Cornell can strengthen this research program and support interdisciplinary and collaborative research ventures. In particular, successful candidates can benefit from association with one or more of Cornell's interdisciplinary research centers, national facilities, and national resources listed at https://www.engineering.cornell.edu/research-and-faculty/centers-and-facilities . While postdoctoral experience is considered to be essential for Assistant Professor applicants, we also encourage applications from candidates nearing the completion of their PhD, in addition to candidates that have postdoctoral experience.  The department can provide additional support in the form of a bridging fellowship for postdoctoral training before the start of the faculty position. How to apply and what to submit: The anticipated starting date is 1 July 2027 or as negotiated.  Interested applicants should submit a cover letter; a curriculum vitae; a publication list; a brief (3-page limit) statement of research interests; a teaching statement; and the names and complete contact information for at least three references: https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32893 . An automated message will be sent to all references requesting letters. Applications received by December 4, 2026 , will be given full consideration. Consistent with Cornell’s practice, appointment to academic positions requires candidates to disclose relevant information about their prior employment including whether they have been found to have violated institutional or employer policies related to or governing unlawful discrimination and harassment, academic and research misconduct, or financial misconduct. Cornell University seeks to meet the needs of dual career couples, has a Dual Career program, and is a member of the Upstate New York Higher Education Recruitment Consortium to assist with dual career searches including positions available in higher education in the upstate New York area. Pay Ranges: $83,200 - $226,200 The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: ● Prior relevant work or industry experience. ● Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. ● Academic Discipline (faculty pay ranges reflects 9-month annual salary) ● Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For specific questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu. If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu. Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-10-05 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    },
    "https://apps.hr.cornell.edu/recruiting/facultyview.cfm?posting_id=_JOB_POSTING-3-111370": {
      "title": "Research Associate- Plowright Laboratory",
      "description": "Academic Career Opportunities Research Associate- Plowright Laboratory Research Associate- Plowright Laboratory The Plowright Lab in the Department of Public and Ecosystem Health at Cornell University seeks applications for a Research Associate. This position is supported through the NIH-funded Center for Spillover Prevention, One Health Research, and Outbreak Readiness in Asia, part of the Centers for Research in Emerging Infectious Diseases (CREID) Network. We study how animals respond to global change and how these responses change the risk of pathogen spillover. The Research Associate will lead, organize, and conduct bat experiments at Cornell University to understand bat immune responses to stress. The Associate will also co-lead field research examining how bats respond to global change and how these responses influence pathogen spillover. This role will include coordinating the ecological components of the fieldwork conducted by local research teams in Indonesia and Bangladesh. The Center’s multidisciplinary team will include colleagues at Johns Hopkins University, the Wildlife Conservation Society, Cornell University College of Veterinary Medicine, Weill Cornell Medicine, Cornell University College of Human Ecology, and Cornell University College of Agriculture and Life Sciences. Responsibilities include: Co-design, plan, and execute bat experiments at Cornell University. Co-lead the ecological components of the field research focused on understanding how bats respond to global change and how these responses influence pathogen spillover. Co-design spatial and temporal sampling strategies in collaboration with geospatial scientists, ecologists, and epidemiologists. Lead analyses and interpretation and the preparation of manuscripts and presentations arising from the experimental and ecological field components, in collaboration with the Center’s team. Help coordinate biomarker development, validation, and field deployment. Represent the research area in cross-team coordination and contribute to the broader research program's scientific direction. Help mentor and support trainees on the team. Contribute to the preparation of donor reports and other project deliverables. Requirements: This position requires a PhD in disease ecology, ecological immunology, epidemiology, veterinary medicine, ecology, or related fields. Demonstrated experience conducting animal or wildlife research; designing experiments; handling and sampling wildlife; and working with infectious-disease or physiological/ecological data. Ability to develop, adapt, and apply quantitative methods to answer ecological questions. A strong record of conducting independent research and scholarly activities, including publication in peer-reviewed journals. Ability to work effectively as part of a multi-disciplinary research team. Ability to manage multiple projects and priorities in a collaborative, fast-paced research environment. Experience in the management of research, scientific, or academic projects. Creative and critical-thinking skills with a demonstrated ability to develop innovative research approaches. Willingness to be based in Ithaca, NY. Ability to travel internationally for fieldwork in Indonesia and Bangladesh (up to one month per year in the field). Preferred: Excellent quantitative skills Experience working with bats Salary and Benefits: Salary Range: $65,000 - $90,000. This position is eligible for endowed benefits. Trainees from the Plowright lab have gone on to positions in academia, government, and non-profit organizations. If you are interested, please email Emma Spence (es957@cornell.edu) for more details. To apply: visit https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32883 and submit a resume with references and their contact information, as well as a paragraph on your unique experiences that would make you suitable for the position (can be formatted as a cover letter). Applications will be reviewed as received, and the position will remain open until filled. Offers higher than the published salary range might be considered to meet unique circumstances, qualifications, academic discipline, and experience. Pay Range: Refer to Posting Language Pay Ranges: The hiring rate of pay for the successful candidate will be determined considering the following criteria: Prior relevant work or industry experience. Education level to the extent education is relevant to the position. Academic Discipline Unique applicable skills. Employment Assistance: For general questions about the position or the application process, please contact the Recruiter listed in the job posting or email mycareer@cornell.edu . If you require an accommodation for a disability in order to complete an employment application or to participate in the recruiting process, you are encouraged to contact Cornell Office of Civil Rights at voice (607) 255-2242, or email at accommodations@cornell.edu . Applicants that do not have internet access are encouraged to visit your local library, or local Department of Labor. You may also request an appointment to use a dedicated workstation in the Office of Talent Attraction and Recruitment, at the Ithaca campus, by emailing mycareer@cornell.edu . Notice to Applicants: Please read the required Notice to Applicants statement by clicking here . This notice contains important information about applying for a position at Cornell as well as some of your rights and responsibilities as an applicant. EEO Statement: Cornell welcomes students, faculty, and staff with diverse backgrounds from across the globe to pursue world-class education and career opportunities, to further the founding principle of “... any person ... any study.” No person shall be denied employment on the basis of any legally protected status or subjected to prohibited discrimination involving, but not limited to, such factors as race, ethnic or national origin, citizenship and immigration status, color, sex, pregnancy or pregnancy-related conditions, age, creed, religion, actual or perceived disability (including persons associated with such a person), arrest and/or conviction record, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, gender expression and/or identity, an individual’s genetic information, domestic violence victim status, familial status, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Cornell University embraces diversity in its workforce and seeks job candidates who will contribute to a climate that supports students, faculty, and staff of all identities and backgrounds. We hire based on merit, and encourage people from historically underrepresented and/or marginalized identities to apply. Consistent with federal law, Cornell engages in affirmative action in employment for qualified protected veterans as defined in the Vietnam Era Veterans’ Readjustment Assistance Act (VEVRAA) and qualified individuals with disabilities under Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act. We also recognize a lawful preference in employment practices for Native Americans living on or near Indian reservations in accordance with applicable law. 2026-09-30 About Us Cornell University is an innovative Ivy League university and a great place to work. Our inclusive community of scholars, students and staff impart an uncommon sense of larger purpose and contribute creative ideas to further the university's mission of teaching, discovery and engagement. With our main campus located in Ithaca, NY, Cornell's far-flung global presence includes the medical college's campuses on the Upper East Side of Manhattan and Doha, Qatar, as well as the Cornell Tech campus located on Roosevelt Island in the heart of New York City. We offer a rich array of services, programs and benefits to help employees advance in their career and enhance the quality of personal life, including: employee wellness, workshops, childcare and adoption assistance, parental leave, flexible work options. @2015 Cornell University"
    }
